course: dictionary
lesson: 10363
title: Travail: hard work and hard times
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound serious and old-fashioned, and travail is one of them. It means very hard, tiring work or effort. You will meet it in formal writing, old books and the King James Bible.
explain: Look for clues around it. The plural travails means the troubles someone goes through. In «the travails of moving house», the words "of moving house" tell you it means the difficult bits of a big job. Try your meaning in the sentence: "the difficult bits of moving house" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Watch out for French. In French, travail is the ordinary word for work. «On 1 May people give little bunches of lily of the valley, le muguet, for good luck; 1 May is also a public holiday, la fête du Travail.» The French words are a clue that this is about France, where the holiday's name uses the French word for work.
fact: Travail means very hard, tiring work or effort.
fact: «Travails» (plural) means difficulties and troubles, as in «the travails of moving house».
fact: In old writing, travail can mean the pain and effort of giving birth, as in «a woman in travail».
fact: In French, «travail» means ordinary work, so it is a false friend: it looks familiar but means something different.
fact: A quick check: swap in "hard work" or "troubles" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: Travail means very hard, tiring work or effort. What does «travail» mean? | A holiday by the sea | Light, fun play | *Very hard, tiring work or effort | A small, quiet room
exercise: «The travails of moving house» are hard to face. What do travails mean here? | *Difficulties and troubles | Happy memories | Boxes and bags | Free time
exercise: The French words «la fête du Travail» name a public holiday on 1 May. What is the holiday about? | Snow | Sport | Gardening | *Work
test: Which phrase uses travail in the old sense of giving birth? | The travails of moving house | A bowl of soup | A long summer walk | *A woman in travail
test: What does «years of travail» most likely mean? | *Years of hard, tiring effort | Years of sleep | Years of silence | Years of holidays
exam: In French, what does travail mean, and why can it trick an English reader? | *Ordinary work, so it can mislead you about the English meaning | A bunch of lily of the valley | Hard, tiring effort, so it is the same as English | A public holiday on 1 May
